class Solution {
  public int prefixCabinetThresholdFile(String[] words, int k) {
    String[] a=words; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {List<Map<Character,Integer>>next=new ArrayList<>();next.add(new HashMap<>());List<Integer>counts=new ArrayList<>();counts.add(0);for(String word:a){int node=0;for(char c:word.toCharArray()){if(!next.get(node).containsKey(c)){next.get(node).put(c,next.size());next.add(new HashMap<>());counts.add(0);}node=next.get(node).get(c);counts.set(node,counts.get(node)+1);}}for(String word:a){int node=0,s=0;for(char c:word.toCharArray()){node=next.get(node).get(c);s+=counts.get(node);}out.add(s);}}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>=k)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build prefixTotals

A prefix is the start of a word. For example, "c", "ca", and "cat" are the prefixes with at least one letter of "cat". For each word in `words`, look at all its prefixes with at least one letter. Count how many input `words` start with each prefix, then add those counts. Count repeated input `words` separately, and include the word itself. Put one total per word in `prefixTotals`, in input order.

Return the answer

Count the numbers in `prefixTotals` that are at least `k` (greater than or equal to `k`). Store the count in `atLeastK`. Complete `prefixCabinetThresholdFile` so it returns `atLeastK`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `prefixTotals` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(total characters); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length; total characters means all word lengths added together. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

